The Nature of Implicit Memory
Implicit vs. Explicit Memory: As we discussed, implicit memories are non-conscious and non-verbal. They are stored as feelings, sensations, and bodily responses, not as a clear narrative with faces and dialogue. The fact that you couldn't see faces but felt the presence of people is very consistent with how an implicit memory of a traumatic event might manifest.

Trauma and the Body: When a person experiences a traumatic event, especially in early childhood, the memory is often stored in the body, not the narrative parts of the brain. The physical sensations you described—the feeling of reliving a moment—are also very common in trauma responses.

